{"id":"5cbf3dfe-54ed-49c5-8106-a6c1f808aa1e","object":"chat.completion","created":1785021605,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import math\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    matrix = case['matrix']\\n    idf = case['idf']\\n    vector = case['vector']\\n    n = len(matrix)\\n    m = len(idf)\\n    if n == 0 or m == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Empty matrix or idf\\\")\\n    for row in matrix:\\n        if len(row) != m:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Inconsistent matrix row length\\\")\\n    if len(vector) != n:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Vector length mismatch\\\")\\n    X = [[matrix[i][j] * idf[j] for j in range(m)] for i in range(n)]\\n    floor = 1e-10\\n    degree = []\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        sum_sq = sum(X[i][j] * X[i][j] for j in range(m))\\n        degree.append(max(sum_sq, floor))\\n    inv_sqrt_deg = [1.0 / math.sqrt(d) for d in degree]\\n    scaled_vec = [vector[i] * inv_sqrt_deg[i] for i in range(n)]\\n    v = [0.0] * m\\n    for j in range(m):\\n        s = 0.0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            s += X[i][j] * scaled_vec[i]\\n        v[j] = s\\n    w = [0.0] * n\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        s = 0.0\\n        for j in range(m):\\n            s += X[i][j] * v[j]\\n        w[i] = s\\n    result = [w[i] * inv_sqrt_deg[i] for i in range(n)]\\n    return {\\\"degree\\\": degree, \\\"result\\\": result}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for DATA-SNAP-02. The case has matrix (list of lists of ints), idf (list of floats), vector (list of floats). We need to apply matrix-free spectral operator? Actually description: \"Multiply each feature column by its supplied IDF. Define each row degree as the squared norm with a positive numerical floor. Apply inverse square-root degree scaling to the input vector. Compute X times X-transpose through two sparse-style matrix-vector products and rescale rows. Return the rounded operator result and degrees without constructing a pairwise matrix.\"\n\nInterpretation: The matrix X is derived from the given matrix (which is binary, rows are documents? Actually matrix shape: rows x columns (features). Each column is a feature across documents. idf is per feature. We multiply each feature column by its IDF: that means for each column j, multiply all entries in that column by idf[j]. So X becomes matrix where X[i][j] = matrix[i][j] * idf[j]. Then define each row degree as squared norm of row (sum of squares) with a floor (positive numerical floor, say 1e-10). Then compute degree scaling: degree = sqrt( row_norm_squared )? Actually \"inverse square-root degree scaling\" means we multiply vector by 1/sqrt(degree). The vector is input of length number of rows. So scaled_vec[i] = vector[i] / sqrt(degree[i]) (if degree[i] > 0 else something, but floor ensures positive).\n\nThen compute X times X-transpose through matrix-vector products: We need to compute operation A = X * X^T (size rows x rows). But we don't construct it. Instead for a given vector y (which will be the scaled_vec? or we need to compute operator on scaled_vec? The description: \"Apply inverse square-root degree scaling to the input vector. Compute X times X-transpose through two sparse-style matrix-vector products and rescale rows.\" Possibly: We compute z = X * (X^T * scaled_vec) i.e., two matvecs: first compute v = X^T * scaled_vec (size columns), then compute w = X * v (size rows). Then rescale rows: result[i] = w[i] / degree[i] (since degree scaling? Actually \"inverse square-root degree scaling\" applied to input, then X*X^T, then rescale rows. Could be: result is (degree^(-1) * X * X^T * degree^(-1) * vector) where degree is diagonal of row sums? Typically for normalized graph Laplacian. But given description: \"Apply inverse square-root degree scaling to the input vector.\" So input vector is scaled by 1/sqrt(degree). Then compute X * X^T * scaled_vec. Then \"rescale rows\" means multiply